theory.intro:
When the region between $y=f(x)$ and the $x$-axis is made of rectangles, triangles, trapezia or parts of a circle, a definite integral can be found from area formulas alone. Area below the $x$-axis counts as negative.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0.5,0) -- (0.5,2.5) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) -- (5.5,-2.5) -- (5.5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.6) -- (0,4.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-0.4,3.4) -- (6,-3);
\node at (1.4,3) {$y=f(x)$};
\node at (1.4,0.7) {$+$};
\node at (4.6,-0.9) {$-$};
\node[below] at (0.5,-0.12) {$a$};
\node[below] at (5.5,-0.12) {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-1,0) rectangle (4,3);
\draw (-1,0) rectangle (4,3);
\draw[->] (-2.6,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-2.4,3) -- (5.4,3);
\node[above] at (4.8,3.05) {$y=3$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use an area formula to evaluate $\int_{-1}^{4}3\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,6) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.6,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (0,7.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-0.5,7) -- (3.8,-1.6);
\node at (2.8,4) {$y=6-2x$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use an area formula to evaluate $\int_{0}^{3}(6-2x)\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.5cm]
\fill[gray!35] (-1,0) -- (-1,2) -- (3,10) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-3.6,0) -- (4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.4) -- (0,13.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-2.4,-0.8) -- (3.8,11.6);
\node at (0.6,9) {$y=2x+4$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.3) -- (\x,-0.3) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10} \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use an area formula to evaluate $\int_{-1}^{3}(2x+4)\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) arc (0:180:3) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-4.6,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (3,0) arc (0:180:3);
\node at (3.1,3.5) {$y=\sqrt{9-x^{2}}$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph of $y=\sqrt{9-x^{2}}$ is the upper half of a circle centred at the origin.
Use an area formula to evaluate $\int_{-3}^{3}\sqrt{9-x^{2}}\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) rectangle (4,-2);
\draw (1,0) rectangle (4,-2);
\draw[->] (-1.6,0) -- (5.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.8) -- (0,2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-1.4,-2) -- (5.6,-2);
\node[below] at (5,-2.1) {$y=-2$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,-2} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use an area formula to evaluate $\int_{1}^{4}(-2)\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) rectangle (6,4);
\draw (2,0) rectangle (6,4);
\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (7.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,5.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-1,4) -- (7.4,4);
\node[above] at (0.8,4.05) {$y=4$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region is the region between $y=4$ and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=6$.
Use it to evaluate $\int_{6}^{2}4\,dx$.

development.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,-2) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (2,0) -- (5,3) -- (5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-2,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4) -- (0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-1.2,-3.2) -- (5.8,3.8);
\node at (4,4) {$y=x-2$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-2,2} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The line $y=x-2$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=2$.
Find the **value of the definite integral** $\int_{0}^{5}(x-2)\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-2,0) -- (-2,-1) -- (-1,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (-1,0) -- (1,2) -- (1,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-4.4,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2) -- (0,4.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-3.4,-2.4) -- (2.4,3.4);
\node at (-2.6,1.6) {$y=x+1$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the **total area enclosed** between $y=x+1$, the $x$-axis, $x=-2$ and $x=1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (4,0) arc (0:90:4) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.4,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,5.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (4,0) arc (0:90:4);
\node at (2.2,4.8) {$y=\sqrt{16-x^{2}}$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph of $y=\sqrt{16-x^{2}}$ is the upper half of a circle centred at the origin.
Use an area formula to evaluate $\int_{0}^{4}\sqrt{16-x^{2}}\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-4,0) -- (-4,4) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (2,2) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-5.8,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,5.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-4.8,4.8) -- (0,0) -- (3.6,3.6);
\node at (2.6,4.6) {$y=|x|$};
\foreach \x in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use area formulas to evaluate $\int_{-4}^{2}|x|\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) -- (1,2) -- (4,5) -- (4,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-2.2,0) -- (6.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.6) -- (0,7.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-1.4,-0.4) -- (5.4,6.4);
\node at (1.4,5.6) {$y=x+1$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region lies between $y=x+1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4$.
Use it to evaluate $\int_{4}^{1}(x+1)\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (2,4) -- (5,4) -- (5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,5.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2,4) -- (5,4);
\node at (3.4,4.8) {$y=f(x)$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph of $y=f(x)$ rises in a straight line from the origin to $(2,4)$, then stays level.
Use area formulas to evaluate $\int_{0}^{5}f(x)\,dx$.

mastery.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.5cm]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,-6) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) -- (5,4) -- (5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.6,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-8.4) -- (0,6.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-0.4,-6.8) -- (5.6,5.2);
\node at (1.6,4) {$y=2x-6$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (\x,0.3) -- (\x,-0.3) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4} \draw (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
For $y=2x-6$ on $0\le x\le 5$, find the value of $\int_{0}^{5}(2x-6)\,dx$, and then find the total area enclosed between the line and the $x$-axis over that interval.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (-5,0) arc (180:90:5) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-6.8,0) -- (2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.2) -- (0,6.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (-5,0) arc (180:90:5);
\node at (-2.4,5.6) {$y=\sqrt{25-x^{2}}$};
\foreach \x in {-5,-4,-3,-2,-1,1} \draw (\x,0.12) -- (\x,-0.12) node[below,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw (0.12,\y) -- (-0.12,\y) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph of $y=\sqrt{25-x^{2}}$ is part of a circle centred at the origin.
Evaluate $\int_{-5}^{0}\sqrt{25-x^{2}}\,dx$, and hence write down the value of $\int_{0}^{-5}\sqrt{25-x^{2}}\,dx$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, y=0.4cm]
\fill[gray!35] (1,0) -- (1,2) -- (4.5,9) -- (4.5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-1.2,0) -- (5.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2) -- (0,12.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-0.6,-1.2) -- (5.5,11);
\node at (1.6,9) {$y=2x$};
\draw (1,0.4) -- (1,-0.4) node[below] {$1$};
\draw (4.5,0.4) -- (4.5,-0.4) node[below] {$k$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (current bounding box.south west) {NOT TO SCALE};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The shaded region lies between $y=2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=k$, where $k>1$.
Given that $\int_{k}^{1}2x\,dx=-24$, find $k$.
